WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.969
-Our next guest is
an award-winning journalist

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.937
and correspondent you know best
from his long-running work

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:08.007
on "Dateline." The 30th season
premieres this Friday,

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:10.042
September 24th,
right here on NBC.

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:13.513
Please welcome to the show
and the studio Keith Morrison.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:15.682
[ Applause ]

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:17.683
-Wow.

00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:19.485
[ Laughs ]

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:21.820
-Welcome back. Thank you
so much for being here.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.055
Really appreciate it.

00:00:23.055 --> 00:00:25.758
Come on up.
-Thank you.

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:28.027
Well, well, well.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:29.496
This is where you live, huh?

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:31.230
-This is where I live.
It's not too bad, right?

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.199
-I thought you were
at that Italian restaurant

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.034
most of the time.
-No, that's not me.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:36.402
You're thinking of someone else.

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:38.371
30 seasons!
-I know.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.673
I know.
-Can you believe it?

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.475
-Believe what?
-That it's 30 seasons?

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.178
-Oh, that. Oh, that. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:47.380
No, I can't,
as a matter of fact.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.181
You know, it goes by
about in a week and a half.

00:00:49.181 --> 00:00:50.383
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:51.517
-When you started --
-Didn't I see you about

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:52.718
a week and a half ago?

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:54.087
-It felt like a week --
You were actually --

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:56.289
The first time you were here,
you were in our studio,

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:57.790
was the first season
of our show.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.525
We did a sketch together
where --

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:00.560
-Which was last week.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.728
-It felt like last week.
-Huh.

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.062
-But it was --
But would you believe

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:04.763
it was almost seven years ago
that you were here?

00:01:04.763 --> 00:01:07.000
Isn't that crazy?
-Amazing. Yeah.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.702
-When did you know
"Dateline" was working?

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.805
-You know, it -- it actually
worked from the very beginning.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:14.140
It worked so well
from the very beginning

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.543
that they wanted the show on
five nights a week.

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:17.876
-Yeah.

00:01:17.876 --> 00:01:20.312
-Even when we didn't do crime,
it worked quite well.

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:22.815
And there were a lot of stories
I loved doing.

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.517
Stories about a whale
or a wolf or...

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.651
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:27.320
-Do you -- Do they
let you do many whale

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.388
or wolf stories these days?

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:30.122
-No whale and
wolf stories anymore, no.

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:31.357
-No. That's too bad.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.925
'Cause, I mean,
I feel like you were at the --

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:34.527
at the forefront
of this true crime --

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:36.296
-Of the whale -- Oh, no.
Of the true crime.

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:38.030
-No, no. The whale.
You guys were way at the end

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.333
of the whale --
the whale moment.

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:41.534
-Sure.

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.469
-But you guys were true crime.
-Yeah, it's true.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:45.438
It's true.
And we discovered it

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:48.807
kind of by accident,
I guess you could say.

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:51.243
But there were -- there were
good stories out there,

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:53.212
which were crime stories.

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:54.780
We did a couple of them.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.349
They did extraordinarily well,

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.650
compared to the rest
of the programs,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.086
so we did more of them.
-Yeah.

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.221
-And then we just made them
longer and longer and longer.

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:04.691
-And the world
has done a nice job

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:07.694
by continuing
to provide you crimes.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:09.161
-They do that.
-Yeah.

00:02:09.161 --> 00:02:11.597
-Yeah. Uh-huh. Yeah.
-And -- [ Chuckles ]

00:02:11.597 --> 00:02:15.268
When you -- When you --
When they bring them to you,

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:18.438
when they say, "Hey, Keith,
here's a new story.

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:19.539
Here's a woman."

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:21.040
It does seem like a lot of wives

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.775
want to get rid
of their husbands.

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.744
Is that -- Would you say
thematically that has --

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:28.314
-You know, I think that's --

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.683
I think that's a fantasy
by some people.

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.785
-Yeah. I think you may -- Maybe
they don't want to act on it,

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:35.054
but they don't mind watching
a "Dateline" or two about it.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.022
-The sad truth, it's usually
the other way around.

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:38.991
So it makes a better story,
you know?

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:40.225
-Right. That's true.

00:02:40.225 --> 00:02:42.128
-It's the dog-bites-man story.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:44.364
Everybody's like, "Finally --
Finally they're getting theirs."

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:47.366
-Yeah.
-Did you know that your voice

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:52.471
would be a perfect fit
for these kinds of stories?

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:55.108
-It is? No. No.
-You didn't know?

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:57.943
-Well, who -- Come on, now.

00:02:57.943 --> 00:02:59.579
What are we doing here, anyway?

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:01.648
No, I didn't actually.
I had no idea.

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:03.783
-Yeah, but that seems to be one
of the nice surprises about it.

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:05.518
-But it's -- it's odd. Yeah.

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:08.354
You know,
people do imitations of it.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:12.325
-Bill Hader did a famous
imitation of you on "SNL."

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:14.293
When we were together
the first time,

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:16.128
I texted him and said,
"I hope you're jealous,

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:18.798
'cause I'm with the real --
the real Keith Morrison."

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:19.866
-Yeah. Uh-huh. Yeah.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.468
-Did you enjoy
his impression of you?

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:23.969
-It was -- Yes.
-Okay.

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:25.571
-I did, and I didn't.

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.373
Have you ever --
Have you had some --

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.041
somebody do
an impression of you?

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:31.277
-Yeah.
-In kind of a parody like that?

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:33.079
-Yeah, but not quite like that.
-Yeah.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:34.713
-
I mean, I feel like he really...

00:03:34.713 --> 00:03:36.682
-So, if the first time
you ever saw it,

00:03:36.682 --> 00:03:38.518
you were unprepared for it

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:40.252
and it was
on national television...

00:03:40.252 --> 00:03:42.721
-Right.
-On "Saturday Night Live."

00:03:42.721 --> 00:03:44.891
And you turn on the TV,
and somebody's doing that.

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.559
-Is that how --
Did you see it first

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.227
or did you get a bunch
of texts from people?

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:50.363
-No. Well, I got --
My daughter texted me.

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:52.398
-Yeah. Was she happy?
Did your daughter like it?

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.400
-She was laughing her head off.
-Okay, well, that's good.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.202
-So --
-I think that's a good sign,

00:03:56.202 --> 00:03:57.737
if your daughter
laughs her head off.

00:03:57.737 --> 00:04:01.607
-Yeah, well -- But it does
a wonderful thing, actually,

00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:04.443
for a person's sense
of him or herself.

00:04:04.443 --> 00:04:05.978
-I think so, too.

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:08.081
-It's a -- It's a --
You know, it's an amazing thing.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.849
It feels -- makes you feel good,

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:12.518
but at the same time, you
realize even when you feel good,

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:16.823
even when you feel noticed and
appreciated, you're an idiot.

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:19.425
You always were an idiot.
-I don't think that's --

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:20.859
I don't think
that's the takeaway.

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:22.995
One thing that I feel like
proves you're not an idiot

00:04:22.995 --> 00:04:25.764
is you --
There's an Instagram account

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:29.101
which is all photos of you
leaning against things.

00:04:29.101 --> 00:04:30.536
-I never lean.

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:32.571
-This is something that you --
-That's fake photography.

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:35.374
-Will you explain why you have
decided to lean against things?

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.209
-I didn't decide.
It was just -- You know --

00:04:37.209 --> 00:04:39.778
-Here. Here's another example.
-Look. You know what happened?

00:04:39.778 --> 00:04:42.081
When people talk --
Have you watched the news?

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:43.916
-I've watched the news
once or twice.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.084
-Once or twice, yeah.

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:47.019
So, they don't do it
quite so much anymore.

00:04:47.019 --> 00:04:49.721
But it used to be
that you'd see some person

00:04:49.721 --> 00:04:50.890
walking along the street,

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.559
gesticulating
with his or her hands

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:54.993
and walking toward a camera

00:04:54.993 --> 00:04:57.530
or walking somewhere
and kind of telling you what --

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.665
And I always thought
that looked ridiculous.

00:04:59.665 --> 00:05:02.334
I liked the people involved,
but why are they doing that?

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:03.802
And so --
-So do you mean, like,

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:05.771
a news story starts
and someone's walking towards

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:07.473
camera, like,
telling you what happened.

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.508
-Uh-huh. Uh-huh.
-And you didn't like it?

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:12.311
-I didn't like it. No.
-So you came up with the lean?

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:14.180
-[ Scoffs ]
What do you mean "came up with"?

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.548
I was leaning to begin with.

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:16.716
-You were leaning
to begin with,

00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:18.117
but I feel like --
-I'm leaning right now.

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:20.052
-But, look --
I -- I mean, look, at this.

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:21.554
This --
I mean, it's such a pro move.

00:05:21.554 --> 00:05:23.756
Like, this guy -- I want to hear
what this guy has to say.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:25.091
-Yeah.
He's leaning on a fence.

00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:26.425
-The guy's leaning
on the fence.

00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:28.360
He's not in a rush.
He wants to tell you a story.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.430
-Barbed wire fence.
-Yeah, that part was weird

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:34.434
to me, that you would choose
to not lean against the post.

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:36.035
-You have no idea
how painful that was.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:38.404
-Yeah. And then --
I remember that episode,

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:40.472
a solitary tear
rolled down your face.

00:05:40.472 --> 00:05:41.740
-That's right.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.809
-As the -- As the barb
cut through your sweater.

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:45.844
-Uh-huh. Yeah.
-This one somebody mocked up.

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:47.647
I like that a good deal.
-No, that's real.

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:49.916
-That's real? That's him?
-That's the only real one.

00:05:49.916 --> 00:05:52.751
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.751 --> 00:05:54.853
-Hey. Will you --
-Makes sense, don't you think?

00:05:54.853 --> 00:05:56.421
-You have -- I feel like

00:05:56.421 --> 00:05:59.158
when I do a tease for our
upcoming guests, they're good.

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:01.594
You know, I think people
are excited to see them.

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:04.096
But I feel like I don't add
the sort of drama...

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:05.431
-Ah.
-...that you could.

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:06.665
Do you mind -- Do you mind

00:06:06.665 --> 00:06:08.200
teasing our guests
for tomorrow night?

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.568
It's John Kerry
and Jim Gaffigan.

00:06:09.568 --> 00:06:11.136
-You have John Kerry on
tomorrow night?

00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:12.571
-That's pretty good, right?
-That is pretty good.

00:06:12.571 --> 00:06:16.375
-So, uh, do you mind?
-I don't mind at all.

00:06:16.375 --> 00:06:19.579
Join us tomorrow, when former
Secretary of State John Kerry

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:23.816
shares a secret
that changes everything.

00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:27.319
And when funny man
Jim Gaffigan swings by,

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:29.087
the laughter stops...

00:06:29.087 --> 00:06:32.291
and the screaming begins.

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:36.395
Tomorrow on
"Late Night with Seth Meyers."

00:06:36.395 --> 00:06:38.464
-See, I think that's
gonna be big for us.

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:40.633
I think that's gonna be
really big for us.

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:42.134
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:42.134 --> 00:06:45.370
You're also -- You're also doing
a lot of podcasting now,

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:46.939
which seems like a perfect fit.
-We are doing podcasts.

00:06:46.939 --> 00:06:48.173
Which is -- Yeah,
they don't have to look

00:06:48.173 --> 00:06:50.409
at my face that way.
You can just talk to them.

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:52.478
-Do people ever recognize you
from your voice first

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.513
on the street?
-All the time, yeah.

00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:56.616
-Yeah.
And what do they say to you?

00:06:56.616 --> 00:06:59.084
-"Why do you talk
that funny way?"

00:06:59.084 --> 00:07:01.354
-Well, that seems like
a very fair question.

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:02.755
30 seasons. Congratulations.

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:04.957
-Thank you very much.
-It's really an accomplishment.

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:06.691
And we really appreciate
you being with us.

00:07:06.691 --> 00:07:07.893
30th season of "Dateline"

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:09.695
premieres this Friday
at 9:00 p.m. here on NBC.

